WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.240
Turn your head, and your eyes stay fixed on the world.

2
00:00:06.980 --> 00:00:08.120
How does the ear do that?

3
00:00:09.033 --> 00:00:10.913
Same switch, new front end.

4
00:00:10.913 --> 00:00:14.273
A semicircular canal is a fluid-filled ring.

5
00:00:15.217 --> 00:00:19.237
In its swelling, hair bundles sit in a gelatinous flap called the cupula.

6
00:00:20.250 --> 00:00:24.910
When the head turns, the fluid lags behind, bends the cupula, and pushes the

7
00:00:24.910 --> 00:00:25.910
bundles.

8
00:00:26.283 --> 00:00:31.523
During everyday head movements, the canal's nerve fibres signal roughly how fast the

9
00:00:31.523 --> 00:00:32.523
head is turning.

10
00:00:33.183 --> 00:00:34.723
They fire even at rest.

11
00:00:35.623 --> 00:00:37.323
Canals on opposite sides pair up.

12
00:00:38.217 --> 00:00:41.937
A turn to the left excites the left canal and inhibits the right.

13
00:00:42.917 --> 00:00:47.637
The canals drive the vestibulo-ocular reflex, which keeps the eyes still in the

14
00:00:47.637 --> 00:00:48.637
world.

15
00:00:48.867 --> 00:00:53.827
In a left turn, the left vestibular nucleus excites the right abducens nucleus.

16
00:00:54.900 --> 00:00:58.660
It drives the right lateral rectus, and the right eye turns right.

17
00:00:59.683 --> 00:01:05.103
Abducens interneurons cross to the left oculomotor nucleus, which turns the left eye

18
00:01:05.103 --> 00:01:06.103
right.

19
00:01:06.333 --> 00:01:08.913
Both eyes turn right as the head turns left.

20
00:01:09.933 --> 00:01:12.373
Now the left inner ear is suddenly destroyed.

21
00:01:13.333 --> 00:01:14.333
The head is still.

22
00:01:14.933 --> 00:01:16.333
What does the brain think is happening?

23
00:01:22.417 --> 00:01:25.177
The right side's resting firing is now unmatched.

24
00:01:26.097 --> 00:01:27.577
The brain reads a turn to the right.

25
00:01:28.550 --> 00:01:32.050
The eyes drift slowly left, with quick jerks back to the right.

26
00:01:32.870 --> 00:01:34.010
The room seems to spin.

27
00:01:34.890 --> 00:01:36.650
That is one mechanism of vertigo.

28
00:01:37.683 --> 00:01:41.203
The otolith organs sense gravity and straight-line acceleration.

29
00:01:42.323 --> 00:01:44.703
Their bundles are capped by a crystal-loaded membrane.

30
00:01:45.817 --> 00:01:49.177
Tilt the head back, and that heavy membrane slides backward.

31
00:01:50.200 --> 00:01:55.140
Accelerate forward, as an aircraft does on its takeoff run, and it slides backward

32
00:01:55.140 --> 00:01:58.860
too. Without a view outside, you can feel tipped back.

33
00:01:59.833 --> 00:02:03.573
So an otolith organ alone cannot tell tilt from acceleration.

34
00:02:04.713 --> 00:02:07.973
A tilt also rotates the head, and the canals feel rotation.

35
00:02:09.133 --> 00:02:13.373
One switch. A wave, a ring of fluid, a heavy membrane.

36
00:02:14.417 --> 00:02:18.617
And circuits that read which cells are active, and how the two sides compare.
